WEBVTT

00:00:04.000 --> 00:00:22.000
Most leaders dread feedback because it feels like judgment about the past. Feedforward is different — it is a practical coaching approach focused on what they can change going forward.

00:00:23.000 --> 00:00:48.000
Steps for feedforward in a session: first, the client picks one behavior they want to improve — just one. Second, they describe that behavior briefly to a partner or the coach. Third, the coach asks others, "What two suggestions do you have for this person to do better in the future?" No discussion of the past is allowed.

00:00:49.000 --> 00:01:14.000
Solution technique: listen to every suggestion without arguing. Say only "Thank you." The client writes down ideas that resonate. After several rounds, they choose one or two behaviors to practice before the next session. That is how you turn insight into action.

00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:40.000
When a client resists accountability, ask stakeholder-centered questions: "Who else is affected by this habit?" and "What would your team notice if you changed?" Link the behavior to relationships they already care about.

00:01:41.000 --> 00:02:05.000
Daily follow-up exercise: at the end of each day, ask "Did I do my best to…?" for each goal behavior. No score above 10 — it is a self-check, not a grade. Over weeks, the pattern of honest answers matters more than any single day.
